    "b-sks-rc-patterns-resolution-heading": {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Resolution moves are patterns"
        }
      ],
      "id": "b-sks-rc-patterns-resolution-heading",
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-rc-patterns-resolution-body": {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "A resolution move — \"mismatches of shape X resolve by Y\" — is not its own table. It is a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pattern",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " entity whose substance is an ordinary fact: a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "value",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " stating the move, a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "rationale",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " arguing for it, a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "confidence",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "evidence_ids",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " pointing at the worker-run submissions where the move worked (the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "attempt://",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " store). Example fact: \"two stores to the same struct swapped in the diff resolve by swapping the source assignments\", its evidence pinning the submissions whose scores rose after the swap. Formerly this knowledge lived in the retired "
        },
        {
          "insert": "tactic",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "tactic_use",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pattern_tactic",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " tables. The raw material stays in the ledger regardless — submission descriptions and hypotheses — so structure can be rebuilt from that data later if a real population emerges."
        }
      ],
      "id": "b-sks-rc-patterns-resolution-body",
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-rc-patterns-consulted-heading": {
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Consulted, not matched"
        }
      ],
      "id": "b-sks-rc-patterns-consulted-heading",
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-rc-patterns-consulted-body": {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Patterns are not lookups, and there is no recognizer machinery: nothing fires them, ranks them, or turns them into a checklist. They are context the librarian reads by judgment while indexing new material — does this look like a convention we already noticed? — and cites only when it thinks the connection is real. Workers see them on the target card as information with provenance, never as a checklist or ranked prescriptions. Builder pipelines may propose "
